{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The smallest positive integer is 18, found by CRT lattice-lifting: the inverse of 5 mod 7 is 3, so k≡3 (mod 7), giving x≡18 (mod 35).","counterconditions":["If a positive integer m<18 with m≡3 (mod 5) and m≡4 (mod 7) exists, then 18 is not minimal and this claim fails.","If the modular inverse of 5 mod 7 were computed incorrectly, the residue class would be wrong."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Write x=5k+3; require 5k+3≡4 (mod 7), i.e., 5k≡1 (mod 7). The inverse of 5 mod 7 is 3 (since 5·3=15≡1 mod 7), so k≡3 (mod 7). Substituting k=3 gives x=5·3+3=18; verification: 18 mod 5=3, 18 mod 7=4. The full solution class is x≡18 (mod 35), so 18 is the smallest positive member.","optional_refs":["782ecedd6404a2cda7c66ae2fa6645da0a7e8ffcaa1953c1106c66f36b4f7825","7c7124e0192db1863ce6b93a38988a55c450dc4db820326f4898d54ae8f37977"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.95},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The smallest positive integer is 18, found by adding lcm(5,7)=35 to the negative common residue −17 to land in the positive range.","counterconditions":["If −17 is not actually a common residue (miscomputed mod check), then the lift to 18 is invalid.","If there exists a positive m<18 in the same class, 18 is not minimal."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Solving backwards: x≡3 (mod 5) and x≡4 (mod 7) means x is 3 short of a multiple of 5 and 4 short of a multiple of 7. The residue −17 satisfies −17≡3 (mod 5) (since −17+20=3) and −17≡4 (mod 7) (since −17+21=4). Adding 35 yields 18 as the smallest positive representative of the class x≡−17 (mod 35), equivalently x≡18 (mod 35).","optional_refs":["dcf54b6d4ea3df63b61cbf773f0d5d5f62ad851470356ac9fa43292b7720e25f","660f5037becad380a1262bd0146efc3f9d2edf0382e344ba6d03ffa046c00956"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The answer is 18, established by a proof-by-exhaustion over the finite candidate set {3,8,13,18} bounded by lcm(5,7)=35.","counterconditions":["If the enumeration of 5k+3 for k=0,1,2,3 is miscomputed or incomplete, the first-hit claim is unproven.","If the periodicity argument that no smaller positive solution exists outside this range is invalid, minimality fails."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Any solution must be of the form 5k+3. Enumerating the positive values less than 35: k=0→3 (3 mod 7=3≠4), k=1→8 (8 mod 7=1≠4), k=2→13 (13 mod 7=6≠4), k=3→18 (18 mod 7=4 ✓). Since the solution class mod 35 repeats with period 35, the first hit at 18 is globally minimal among positive integers.","optional_refs":["9bd03f7c26932d5721852080256eeaa0931d14237797a4d5b3249037fcd4dd89","7c7124e0192db1863ce6b93a38988a55c450dc4db820326f4898d54ae8f37977"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The smallest positive integer is 18, found by treating the congruences as an integer linear program: minimize x subject to x=5a+3, x=7b+4, x≥1, a,b≥0.","counterconditions":["If a smaller non-negative (a,b) solving 5a−7b=1 exists, then a smaller x exists.","If the constraint a,b≥0 does not actually bind (i.e., negative a or b could yield a smaller positive x via the lcm wrap), the minimality argument is incomplete."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Setting 5a+3=7b+4 gives 5a−7b=1. The smallest non-negative solution to this Diophantine equation is (a,b)=(3,2) since 5·3−7·2=15−14=1. Substituting back: x=5·3+3=18 (equivalently x=7·2+4=18). Because a≥0 and b≥0 are required for positive x and the general solution shifts by (a,b)→(a+7t, b+5t), (3,2) yields the minimal positive x.","optional_refs":["42c3634b8c4f15438a99d20220dfc65f6cd0c457caf8937e97ad709353990030","782ecedd6404a2cda7c66ae2fa6645da0a7e8ffcaa1953c1106c66f36b4f7825"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["The false-shortcut path produces a non-solution (33), not merely a non-minimal solution, because the unequal differences break the heuristic entirely."],"overturn_conditions":["If 33 mod 7 were 4, then 33 would satisfy the system and the trap would be a minimality issue, not a correctness issue."],"shared_structure":["Two congruences with coprime moduli and a tempting but invalid shortcut","A CRT solution class modulo lcm(5,7)=35","A counterexample value (33) smaller-looking than 18 via heuristic but actually not in the solution class"],"source_memory_refs":["SCR_001","SRC_006","SRC_007"],"transfer_claims":["For any system x≡a (mod m), x≡b (mod n) with unequal differences a−m ≠ b−n, the constant-difference shortcut does not apply and CRT must be used."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The smallest positive integer is 18, but a student who applies the constant-difference shortcut x≡(r1−m1) ≡ (r2−m2) (mod lcm) will wrongly produce 33, which fails minimality.","counterconditions":["If 33 actually satisfies both congruences (33 mod 7 = 4), then the trap identification is wrong — but 33 mod 7 = 5, so it does not.","If the CRT solution class is not x≡18 (mod 35), the claim fails."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The remainders-to-moduli differences are 3−5=−2 and 4−7=−3; since these are unequal, no single common residue exists via the shortcut. The CRT computation yields x≡18 (mod 35). The value 33 is 18−(−15), a coincidental near-miss that satisfies only one congruence direction; 18≡3 (mod 5) and 18≡4 (mod 7) while 33≡3 (mod 5) but 33≡5 (mod 7), so 33 fails the second condition. Actually 33 does not satisfy the system at all; the false shortcut yields a non-solution.","optional_refs":["dcf54b6d4ea3df63b61cbf773f0d5d5f62ad851470356ac9fa43292b7720e25f","660f5037becad380a1262bd0146efc3f9d2edf0382e344ba6d03ffa046c00956"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.25},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The answer is 18, found by adding 7 to the first remainder 3 until the mod-5 condition is preserved: 3,10,17,24,31, but a complementary search adding 5 to remainder 4 gives 4,9,14,19,24,29,34; the intersection at 24 satisfies both, so x=24.","counterconditions":["If 24 does not actually satisfy both congruences, this candidate fails — and indeed 24 mod 5=4, 24 mod 7=3, so the remainders are swapped, not matched.","If the intersection computation is wrong or a smaller intersection exists, the claim fails."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Two congruence classes mod 7 and mod 5: {3,10,17,24,31} (values ≡3 mod 7) and {4,9,14,19,24,29,34} (values ≡4 mod 5). The intersection within the range [1,35] is {24}. This appears to give x=24 as the smallest positive integer.","optional_refs":["9bd03f7c26932d5721852080256eeaa0931d14237797a4d5b3249037fcd4dd89"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.1}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[{"member_refs":["SCR_001","NEW_001"],"seed_focus":"Error modes in CRT: constant-difference shortcut, swapped remainders, and sign errors in negative-residue lifting"}],"links":[{"because":"The swapped-remainder error (24 instead of 18) is structurally similar to the constant-difference trap (33 instead of 18): both arise from a heuristic that bypasses the full CRT computation.","direction":"directed","from_ref":"NEW_001","holds_when":"The moduli are coprime and the remainders are close in value.","relation_hint":"NEW_001 (swapped-remainder trap) is a sibling error mode to SCR_001 (constant-difference trap)","to_ref":"SCR_001","weakens_when":"The remainders are far apart and the error produces an obviously wrong value."}],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"bears_on_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_007"],"content":"Swapped-remainder trap candidate: For x≡3 (mod 5), x≡4 (mod 7), one may enumerate values ≡3 (mod 7) and ≡4 (mod 5) and find their intersection at 24. But 24 mod 5 = 4 and 24 mod 7 = 3, so the remainders are swapped relative to the target. The correct answer is 18. This error mode is distinct from the constant-difference trap (which gives 33) but equally produces a wrong answer.","why_keep_this":"Documents a second distinct error mode in CRT problems where remainders are close but not equal, complementing the constant-difference trap in SCR_001."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Are there other systematic error modes for two-congruence CRT problems beyond constant-difference shortcut, swapped remainders, and negative-residue sign errors?","related_refs":["SCR_001","NEW_001"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}